    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Install_Vanilla_Or_The_Fabric_Forge_Neof">Install vanilla or the Fabric / Forge / NeoForge client loaders. Anything you install appears in the Installed versions list automatically.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_A_Modpack_Overwrites_The_Mods_And_Config">A modpack overwrites the mods and config folders in the target directory. Installing into a fresh version folder is recommended so it doesn't get mixed in with your existing mods.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Changes_The_Launcher_S_Colour_Family_Eac">Changes the launcher's colour family. Each family has both a light and a dark variant; which one you see is decided by the mode button on the home page or by the auto cycle below.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Takes_You_From_Account_To_Launch_In_One_">Takes you from account to launch in one pass. Every step — mods, resource packs, shaders — can be skipped, or you can import a modpack and skip the selection steps entirely.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_2_Create_A_Tcp_Tunnel_And_Set_The_Local_">2. Create a TCP tunnel and set the local port to your server's port (25565 by default — check the address shown on the server's card in the Server list).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_1_Sign_In_To_Your_Router_S_Admin_Page_Us">1. Sign in to your router's admin page (usually 192.168.1.1 or 192.168.0.1 in a browser — check the label on the back of the router).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Resource_Packs_Shader_Packs_Data_Packs_W">Resource packs, shader packs, data packs, worlds and modpacks are all .zip files, and the contents didn't make it clear which this one is. Picking wrong puts it in the wrong folder, so we're asking.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Bedrock_Dedicated_Server_Is_Published_Fr">Bedrock Dedicated Server is published free of charge on Mojang's own site. No account or licence is needed, so it can be downloaded directly.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Switches_Between_Light_And_Dark_Automati">Switches between light and dark automatically based on the system clock (light from 08:00 to 19:00 by default, dark otherwise). The switching times can be adjusted in Settings.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_In_Game_Watermark_Text_Shown_In_The_Bott">In-game watermark text shown in the bottom-left corner, e.g. &amp;quot;XCL2&amp;quot;. Leave blank to keep the original &amp;quot;release&amp;quot;.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Turning_This_Off_Restores_The_Old_Behavi">Turning this off restores the old behaviour: the modpack is merely extracted over an existing instance, with no game or loader downloaded. If the versions don't match, the game will simply crash.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Installing_The_Fabric_Client_Doesn_T_Run">Installing the Fabric client doesn't run a local installer, so you can leave Java blank here. The right Java is still matched or downloaded automatically when you launch.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Keep_Resource_Data_And_Shader_Packs_Sepa">Keep resource, data and shader packs separate per version (on by default). Unticking shares a single copy to avoid duplicate downloads. Individual versions can override this on the Versions page.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Already_Have_A_Minecraft_Folder_From_The">Already have a .minecraft folder — from the official launcher or another one? Point at it, skip the download, and it will launch once Java is matched automatically.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Keep_Mods_Resource_Packs_Data_Packs_And_">Keep mods, resource packs, data packs and worlds separate per version by default (recommended — the same behaviour as the official launcher). Individual versions can override this on the Versions page.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Colour_Themes_Blue_Yellow_Purple_Pink_An">Colour themes (blue, yellow, purple, pink and so on) are now a stable feature rather than an experimental one. Use them directly in Settings — you don't need to come here.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Pick_A_Java_For_This_Server_From_The_Lis">Pick a Java for this server from the list registered in Settings. Choosing "(unset — auto-detect/download)" uses the path box below and matches or downloads one as needed at creation time.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Normal_Mode_Shows_Only_The_Essentials_An">Normal mode shows only the essentials and detects or downloads Java for you, with nothing to configure. Expert mode exposes the Java version, architecture and install type, custom launch arguments and other advanced options.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Sign_In_With_A_Third_Party_Auth_Server_A">Sign in with a third-party auth server account (such as blessing-skin or a unified pass — it must support the Yggdrasil protocol). Your skin from that server is used automatically.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Quickly_Checks_The_Usual_Places_For_Inst">Quickly checks the usual places for installed Java — the portable folder, JAVA_HOME, the registry and PATH — and adds them all to the list below. It doesn't scan the whole disk and finishes in seconds.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Switches_Between_Dark_And_Light_It_Only_">Switches between dark and light. It only changes brightness, not the colour family chosen under Colour theme. With auto cycle on, this is a temporary override — the cycle takes back over at the next switching time.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_This_Feature_Is_Locked_By_Default_And_Ne">This feature is locked by default and needs the correct token to continue (not case-sensitive). No token? Then it isn't meant for you yet — stick to the stable parts of the launcher for now.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Once_Several_Java_Runtimes_Are_Registere">Once several Java runtimes are registered, you can choose which one each client version uses on the Versions page, and which one each server uses when creating or editing it.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Separate_Arguments_With_Spaces_And_Wrap_">Separate arguments with spaces and wrap values containing spaces in double quotes. They're inserted after the launcher's built-in arguments and before the classpath; yours win on any conflict. Leave blank to disable.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_A_Loader_Forked_From_Fabric_It_Runs_Most">A loader forked from Fabric. It runs most Fabric mods and adds some extra features for mod developers. It installs the same way as Fabric and needs no local Java.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Enables_The_Auto_Cycle_Button_On_The_Mai">Enables the auto cycle button on the main screen: light and dark switch automatically at the times set below. You can still override manually with the mode button, but the cycle takes back over at the next switching time.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Blank_Means_Auto_Match_The_Version_S_Own">Blank means auto-match: the version's own requirement and the requirements of installed mods are read, and the higher of the two wins. Enter a number such as 25 to pin this version to that Java major version.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Search_By_Keyword_In_English_Or_Chinese_">Search by keyword in English or Chinese — &amp;quot;JEI&amp;quot;, for example. Leave blank to browse popular mods. Searching happens as you type.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Note_The_Bedrock_Client_Is_Distributed_T">Note: the Bedrock client is distributed through the Microsoft Store and tied to your account's licence. There is no public download URL, so the launcher can only start the copy you already have — it can't download it or switch versions for you.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Supports_Mcworld_Worlds_Mcpack_Resource_">Supports .mcworld (worlds), .mcpack (resource and behaviour packs), .mcaddon (add-ons) and .mctemplate (world templates). You can also drag these files straight onto the launcher window.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_3_The_Tunnelling_Tool_Gives_You_A_Public">3. The tunnelling tool gives you a public address like xxx.xxx.com:12345. Send it to your friends and have them add it under Multiplayer → Add Server in Minecraft.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Removes_Temporary_Files_From_This_Minecr">Removes temporary files from this .minecraft folder that are safe to delete: archived logs, old crash reports, web cache, Mixin debug output and JVM crash dumps. Worlds, mods, resource packs and settings are left alone.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Security_Note_Whichever_Method_You_Use_T">Security note: whichever method you use to expose the server, turning on Online mode under Server settings is recommended so strangers can't wander in. If you're only playing with people you know, a whitelist limits it further.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Experimental_Features_May_Also_Include_J">Experimental features may also include jokes, half-finished work and things that simply haven't been validated yet. That isn't about stability — it's that no decision has been made about shipping them, so they can be cut or changed drastically at any time. Go in expecting that.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Experimental_Features_Currently_Include_">Experimental features currently include low-level things such as multi-loader installs — putting several of Fabric, Forge and NeoForge into one version at once. Used carelessly these can break the version and force a re-download, so make sure you understand them first.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_When_On_The_Launch_Button_Always_Uses_A_">When on, the Launch button always uses a temporary offline account that lasts only for this session, and never reads or writes the real accounts saved on the Accounts page. Closing the launcher clears this session's game logs and temporary downloads; the installed game, mods and worlds are untouched.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Drag_Mods_Resource_Packs_Shaders_Worlds_">Drag mods, resource packs, shaders, worlds or modpacks straight onto the launcher window to install them. Resource packs, shaders, data packs, worlds and modpacks are all .zip files, so the launcher looks inside to work out which is which and only falls back to the settings below when it genuinely can't tell.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_3_Friends_Connect_Using_Your_Public_Ip_A">3. Friends connect using your public IP and port. If that IP changes — most home connections are dynamic — pair it with a dynamic DNS service so a fixed name always points at your current address, and you don't have to tell everyone each time it changes.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_The_Toolbox_Advancement_Images_Skin_Avat">The Toolbox — advancement images, skin avatars, custom downloads, premium skin downloads, junk cleanup, shortcuts, launch counts, standalone loader JARs, memory optimisation — is a stable feature, not an experimental one. It's always available under Tools in the sidebar.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_2_Find_Port_Forwarding_Port_Mapping_Or_V">2. Find "port forwarding", "port mapping" or "virtual server" (the name varies by brand) and add a rule: set both the external and internal port to your server's port (25565 by default), and the internal IP to this computer's address on your local network — you'll find it in your system settings or the router's connected-devices list.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_A_Tunnelling_Tool_Gives_You_A_Temporary_">A tunnelling tool gives you a temporary public address and forwards traffic from it to the server port on your machine. There's no router configuration and no public IP required, and it takes minutes to set up — ideal for playing with friends now and then. The catch is that free tiers usually cap bandwidth and connections, so it's less suited to running a server long-term than the router option below.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_For_Reference_Java_8_Covers_Versions_Bef">For reference: Java 8 covers versions before 1.16, Java 17 before 1.20.5, Java 21 before 1.21.11, and Java 25 from 26.1 onwards. Java 8 defaults to 32-bit and the rest to 64-bit — use your judgement for anything unusual.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_Rent_A_Cheap_Cloud_Server_It_Comes_With_">Rent a cheap cloud server — it comes with a public IP — and use something like frp to relay traffic from your local server through it, or simply run the whole server on the cloud machine instead. This is usually faster and more reliable than free tunnelling, but it costs money and takes more setup, so it suits long-running servers where latency matters.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Ui_If_Your_Connection_Has_A_Public_Ipv4_Or_">If your connection has a public IPv4 or IPv6 address — ask your ISP, or check the WAN status page on your router for a private address such as 100.x, 10.x or 192.168.x, which means you're behind carrier NAT and would need to request a public IP or use option 1 or 3 instead — you can forward the public port to this machine's server port on the router. No third-party service and no bandwidth cap, but it takes a bit more setting up than tunnelling.</system:String>
